            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>First Clarify Your Budgeting Stage</h2>
<p>There's no one-size-fits-all answer to ad budget allocation, but there is a framework. The logic differs entirely between cold start, scaling, and stable growth phases. Copying someone else's ratios might just burn your budget for a week.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Execution Checklist: DTC Budget Allocation SOP</h2>
<h3>Step 1: Identify Your Current Phase</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Phase</th>
<th>Monthly Ad Spend (Reference)</th>
<th>Core Objective</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Cold Start</td>
<td>&lt; $3,000/month</td>
<td>Find profitable ad sets</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Scaling</td>
<td>$3,000 - $20,000/month</td>
<td>Scale high-performing channels</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Stable Growth</td>
<td>&gt; $20,000/month</td>
<td>Maintain ROAS while expanding</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checkpoint</strong>: Write down your current phase and monthly budget. Keep it visible.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 2: Allocate Budget by Platform</h3>
<h4>Primary Channels (40-60% of Total Budget)</h4>
<p><strong>Facebook / Instagram (Meta Ecosystem)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Best for: Fast-moving consumer goods, fashion, homeware, tools</li>
<li>Allocation: 50% at startup, maintain at 40% during scaling</li>
<li>Testing tip: Run at least 3 ad sets, $15-30/day each. Avoid underfunding single ad sets, which triggers repeated learning phases.</li>
</ul>
<h4>Secondary Channels (20-30% of Total Budget)</h4>
<p><strong>Google / YouTube</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Best for: High-intent keywords, category terms, competitor terms; visually-driven products</li>
<li>Allocation: 60% Search, 40% P-Max or Shopping</li>
<li>Case study: A utility app used Google to bid on competitor keywords, achieving ROAS of 5.2. Main purpose was capturing branded search re-purchases from Meta's low-cost traffic.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>TikTok</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Best for: Younger demographics, lower AOV, visually appealing products</li>
<li>Allocation: Build a separate TikTok budget pool during scaling. Don't mix with Meta for clean data isolation.</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h3>Step 3: Testing Phase Budget Details</h3>
<p><strong>New Creatives Testing (20% of Total Budget)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Minimum per creative: $50/day × 5 days = $250 per set</li>
<li>Retention criteria: CTR &gt; 1.5%, CVR &gt; 3%</li>
<li>Pause underperformers promptly to avoid wasted spend.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>New Channel Testing (10-15% of Single Channel Budget)</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Change only one variable at a time: platform OR audience OR creative, never simultaneously.</li>
<li>Run for a full 7 days before judging. Avoid early termination.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Checkpoint</strong>: Is your testing budget being "eaten up"? Check if too much money is stuck in main ad sets with nothing left for creative testing.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h3>Step 4: Daily/Weekly Budget Adjustment Checklist</h3>
<h4>Daily Check (5 minutes)</h4>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Did you reduce budget or pause ad sets with ROAS &lt; 1.5 yesterday?</li>
<li>[ ] Is today's budget sufficient for primary ad sets?</li>
<li>[ ] Any anomalies like an ad set hitting its cap early?</li>
</ul>
<h4>Weekly Check (20 minutes)</h4>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Compare CAC across channels: Meta / Google / TikTok—which is most expensive?</li>
<li>[ ] Do high-performing ad sets need budget increases?</li>
<li>[ ] Can you reallocate 10-20% from underperforming channels to test new directions?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h3>Step 5: Budget Reallocation Decision Thresholds</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Situation</th>
<th>Recommended Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Single ad set ROAS &gt; 1.5× target for 3 consecutive days</td>
<td>Increase budget 20-30%, observe for 3 days</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Single channel overall ROAS below threshold for 5 consecutive days</td>
<td>Reduce that channel's budget by 15%, observe for 1 week</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Creative CTR &lt; 0.5% AND CVR &lt; 1%</td>
<td>Pause. Don't get emotionally attached.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Total budget nearly spent but ROAS is on target</td>
<td>Add budget to high-performing channels first. Don't spread evenly.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>FAQ</h2>
<p><strong>Q: Should budget be concentrated or diversified?</strong></p>
<p>Concentrate early. Test one channel-ad set combination until profitability, then diversify. Diversifying too early means every ad setstarves for sufficient learning budget.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to allocate across multiple site accounts?</strong></p>
<p>Rank by historical ROAS. High-ROAS sites get higher budget coefficients, but each site needs at least $500/month testing budget during cold start.</p>
<p><strong>Q: Should I lock in inventory before peak season?</strong></p>
<p>Meta and Google ad inventory gets competitive during peak seasons. Gradually increase budget 2 weeks before events—don't spike on the day itself, or CPMs will soar.</p>
<hr />
<h2>One-Line Takeaway</h2>
<p>Budget allocation is dynamic. Let data guide you—scale channels that perform, and stop relying on gut feelings for decisions.</p>
